    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d674">

                <pb facs="0848" n="758" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg758"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72113/1329: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McMillin</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-10-04T17:00:00-06:36"><hi rendition="#italic">October 4,
                                1920—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 5—1:35
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>138. Your instructions number 399 of July 31. The program concerning German
                    properties this Government inclines to carry out is as follows: <list>
                        <item>First, confirm rights of Americans in electric properties purchased by
                            them.</item>
                        <item>Second, protect Apparicios<anchor n="18" xml:id="d674-f1"/> and all
                            other Americans owning interests in German properties.</item>
                        <item>Third, indemnify Guatemala for guarding bridge and other expenses and
                            damages by appropriating the funds accumulated from operating German
                            properties since their seizure amounting to about two million
                            dollars.</item>
                        <item>Four, return said properties except electric light and Apparicio
                            holdings to Germans minus accumulations indicated.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>This program known to the Government and this Legation only and will be changed
                    if not acceptable to the United States. In view of Department’s instruction
                    number 399 of July 31, I shall not oppose this settlement without further
                    instructions and it will be disclosed to the British and the French Legations
                    and taken up with the Germans.</p>
                <p>The Government prefers to conclude the affair before receiving the German
                    Minister.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">McMillin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d674-f1" n="18" xml:id="d674-f1-content">
                <p>Members of the Apparicio family, who were American citizens.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
